.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1206 PM PDT Mon Sep 14 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

Today will be the warmest day of the upcoming week with a slight
cooling trend kicking off tomorrow. The upper level trough that
brought us cooler weather last week has progressed eastward towards
the Intermountain West. In its place, zonal flow has developed over
Central California with slight troughing to redevelop Tuesday. The
transition from more zonal flow to slight troughing will see
temperatures cool 2-5 degrees across the region. This will bring
interior temperatures down from the mid to upper 80s today to the
low to mid 80s tomorrow. Coastal areas stay cooler with highs in the
60s to 70s.

Moderate offshore winds were observed along the ridgeline of the
Vaca Range early this morning but have since been trending
downwards. Mt. St. Helena (elevation of 4340 ft) reported a NNE gust
of 40 mph at 2:40 AM which was the strongest wind gust observed from
this event. The SFO-WMC gradient peaked at -6.10 around 7AM this
morning and has since dropped to -4.90 as of 10AM. It is forecast to
shift positive (onshore) by late this afternoon with light onshore
winds returning everywhere. The return of onshore winds should help
marine stratus to redevelop/move inland tonight. The only caveat to
this is we currently have a dry airmass over us which could keep
stratus development limited for one more night. Breezy onshore winds
redevelop Tuesday afternoon and evening with gusts to around 20-30
mph most likely in the East Bay Valleys, Salinas Valley, higher
elevations, and directly along the coast.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1206 PM PDT Mon Sep 14 2026
(Wednesday through next Sunday)

Troughing becomes more dominant by Wednesday as another upper level
low pushes into the Oregon/California border. We are not
anticipating any rain or thunderstorms for this system across our
region but it will bring an unusually cold airmass across our
region. High temperatures Wednesday and Thursday will be running 5-6
degrees below normal across the lower elevations and closer to 10-15
degrees below normal in the higher elevations and along the coast.
This will keep interior temperatures securely in the 70s and coastal
temperatures in the low to mid 60s. Favored hotspots across the
interior Bay Area and Central Coast will reach the low 80s. The
upper level low deepens and tries to become cut-off from the jet
stream Thursday into Friday. Fortunately, it is a quick moving
system and maintains a neutral tilt. If it took a more positive tilt
and dug in more over NE California/Nevada then it would be more
reminiscent of an inside slider which would raise offshore wind
concerns. As it stands right now, this system will just bring a
round of gusty onshore winds Wednesday into Thursday with gusts
peaking between 20 to 30 mph. Locally higher gusts are possible at
favored windy locations (Altamont Pass, higher elevations, etc.).
The passage of the upper level trough will allow the marine layer to
deepen and see a return of our nightly/early morning stratus along
the coast and portions of the interior.

Troughing influence wanes Friday into the weekend as weak ridging
builds in over California. This will see a gradual return to more
seasonal temperatures in the low to mid 80s across the interior, 60s
to 70s along the coast, and upper 80s to low 90s across favored hot
spots in the Bay Area and interior Central Coast. Even though the
ridge is weak, it will still compress the marine layer and most
likely result in less stratus cover across the interior.

